         \n            <section>\n                <h2>⚠️ Warnings</h2>\n                \n                <div class=\"warning\">Detected 1 front matter and 0 back matter regions</div>\n                \n            </section>\n            \n        </div>\n\n        <div id=\"chapters\" class=\"tab-content\">\n        <section>\n            <h2>📑 Chapter Guide</h2>\n\n            \n\n            <h3 style=\"margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--accent-soft);\">Chapters</h3>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 1</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">15,051 words &middot; 1h 40m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with the narrator, Nick Carraway, arriving by car at a modest house in West Egg, renting it for eighty dollars a month beside the opulent mansions of Daisy Buchanan and the enigmatic Gatsby. He drives across the bay to East Egg for dinner at Daisy and Tom Buchanan’s Georgian Colonial, where Daisy’s ethereal charm, Tom’s aggressive dominance, and Jordan Baker’s silent presence establish an undercurrent of unease, culminating in Daisy accusing Tom of bruising her knuckle. That evening on the rosy porch, Tom delivers a racist monologue citing The Rise of the Coloured Empires, while Daisy, after a phone call, calls Nick 'an absolute rose' and later confesses she hopes her daughter will be a 'beautiful little fool'—a moment undercut by her smirk. Tom then takes Nick by train to the valley of ashes, introducing him to his mistress, Myrtle Wilson, at her husband George’s garage; they travel to New York by train, where Myrtle buys a $10 Airedale puppy and they ride in a lavender taxi to her apartment on 158th Street. Inside the overcrowded apartment, Myrtle, Catherine, and the McKees arrive, and after drinking whisky, Myrtle recounts her affair with Tom and Catherine falsely claims Daisy is Catholic to prevent divorce. The party descends into chaos when Tom breaks Myrtle’s nose after she shouts Daisy’s name, prompting a bloody, drunken scramble; Nick exits with Mr. McKee in the elevator, only to find himself later standing beside Gatsby’s bed, hearing him murmur, before waking alone at dawn in Pennsylvania Station. Meanwhile, at a late-night party at Gatsby’s mansion, Nick and Jordan search for the host, discover the drunken Owl Eyes verifying Gatsby’s real books, and meet Gatsby himself—struck by his rare smile. Gatsby speaks privately with Jordan, leaving her cryptic, as the party collapses into drunken fights and marital altercations. The night ends with Gatsby being called away by a Philadelphia phone call. The next morning, Nick witnesses a car crash outside Gatsby’s gate: a new coupé has lost a wheel, and two men emerge—one bewildered Owl Eyes claiming he wasn’t driving, the other dazed and asking if they ran out of gas—while Gatsby stands alone on the porch under a faint moon. In midsummer, Nick reflects on his daily routine as a clerk in New York, walking home past the city’s nightlife, reconnecting with Jordan, whom he discovers is dishonest after she lies about damaging a borrowed car and he recalls her scandalous golf tournament cheating; their conversation about driving reveals her careless attitude, and Nick concludes with his claim of being one of the few honest people he knows.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Nick Carraway</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Daisy Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Jordan Baker</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Myrtle Wilson</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">George Wilson</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Catherine</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Mr. McKee</span>\n                    \n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">+3 more</span>\n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 2: IV</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">763 words &middot; 5m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On a Sunday morning as church bells ring along the shore, Gatsby’s mansion becomes the center of a lavish, chaotic party attended by a sprawling array of guests from East Egg, West Egg, and New York. The narrator recalls names from a disintegrating timetable dated July 5th, 1922, listing aristocrats, movie producers, gamblers, and socialites—many arriving by automobile—who accept Gatsby’s hospitality while knowing nothing about him. Guests include the Chester Beckers, the Blackbucks, the Snells (who was run over by a car after drunkenness), Klipspringer (known as 'the boarder'), and a rotating group of identical young women accompanying Benny McClenahan. The atmosphere is glittering yet hollow, marked by gossip, drunkenness, and idle speculation, such as the rumor that Gatsby killed a man connected to Von Hindenburg and the devil.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Chester Beckers</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Leeches</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Bunsen</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Doctor Webster Civet</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Hornbeams</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Willie Voltaires</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Blackbuck clan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Ismays</span>\n                    \n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">+60 more</span>\n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 3</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">8,926 words &middot; 59m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On a late July morning, Gatsby arrives at the narrator’s door in his luxurious cream-colored car with nickel accents and a three-noted horn, inviting him to lunch and driving him to Manhattan, during which he delivers a meticulously rehearsed, implausible biography claiming Oxford education, European aristocratic travels, and wartime heroism in the Argonne Forest, backed by a Montenegrin medal and an Oxford photograph; he avoids a traffic stop with a card, hinting at hidden influence, and reveals he intends to make a significant request involving Jordan Baker. At noon in a cellar restaurant on Forty-second Street, Gatsby introduces the narrator to Meyer Wolfshiem, a grotesque underworld figure with human molar cuff buttons, who recounts the murder of Rosy Rosenthal and later reveals Gatsby fixed the 1919 World Series, shocking the narrator; Gatsby abruptly departs before the narrator can introduce him to Tom Buchanan. On a quiet October morning in 1917, Jordan recalls encountering Daisy Fay in her white roadster with Lieutenant Jay Gatsby in Louisville, where his intense gaze foreshadowed their romance; years later, Daisy marries Tom Buchanan after a drunken wedding-day breakdown during which she clutches a letter and tries to return the three-hundred-fifty-thousand-dollar pearl necklace, eventually settling in Chicago with a spotless reputation until Jordan mentions Gatsby’s name and Daisy recognizes him as the officer from her past. After a twilight drive through Central Park, Jordan reveals Gatsby bought his mansion solely to be near Daisy and wants the narrator to invite her to tea; the day before, Gatsby sends a man to cut the grass and delivers an enormous floral arrangement. When Daisy arrives in a large open car during a heavy rain, Gatsby is pale and trembling, nearly knocking over a clock, and initially calls the reunion a 'terrible mistake'—but after the narrator leaves briefly, he returns to find Gatsby glowing with joy and Daisy in tears; Gatsby leads them on foot from the narrator’s house to his mansion via the road and postern gate, displaying his opulent rooms and extravagant shirt collection, which moves Daisy to tears. Later, as rain obscures Daisy’s dock, Gatsby points out the now-diminished green light, shows her photographs of Mr. Dan Cody and his youth in yachting gear, and clippings about her; interrupted by a phone call about a guest from Detroit, he summons Klipspringer to play piano, performing off-key songs as a pink-and-gold sunset appears and Daisy whimsically wishes to enclose Gatsby in a cloud; Gatsby’s face grows bewildered as he realizes Daisy may not fulfill his dream, yet he clutches her hand, captivated by her voice, while the narrator departs alone into the rain, leaving them isolated.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Narrator</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Jordan Baker</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Daisy Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Meyer Wolfshiem</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Rosy Rosenthal</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Mr. Dan Cody</span>\n                    \n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">+3 more</span>\n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 4: VI</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">4,036 words &middot; 26m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter opens with a New York reporter arriving at Gatsby’s West Egg mansion one morning, seeking a statement after hearing rumors about him, while the narrative shifts to reveal Gatsby’s origins as James Gatz of North Dakota, who at seventeen rowed out to Dan Cody’s yacht, the Tuolomee, on Lake Superior, impressing Cody with his ambition and spending five years aboard in roles from steward to jailor; after Cody’s death, legal maneuvers by Ella Kaye deprived Gatsby of his $25,000 legacy, solidifying his self-invented identity as Jay Gatsby, shaped by Cody’s influence and his aversion to alcohol. Later, after a weeks-long absence, the narrator visits Gatsby’s mansion on a Sunday afternoon and is startled to find Tom Buchanan, Mr. Sloane, and a woman in a brown riding-habit arriving on horseback; Gatsby, visibly nervous, insists on serving drinks and invites them to supper, but Sloane and the woman subtly refuse and depart abruptly. That week, Tom and Daisy attend Gatsby’s party, where Daisy is captivated yet unsettled by the raw extravagance of West Egg, and Gatsby dances with her, desperately hoping she will declare she never loved Tom to erase five years and restart their past—she remains emotionally distant. As the night ends, Gatsby confides in the narrator his belief that he can repeat the past, recalling the moonlit night five years prior when he first kissed Daisy, convinced he had captured something eternal.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">James Gatz</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Dan Cody</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Ella Kaye</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Reporter</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Narrator</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Mr. Sloane</span>\n                    \n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">+2 more</span>\n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 5: VII</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">8,766 words &middot; 58m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On the hottest day of summer, the narrator arrives by train at the Buchanans’ house to find Gatsby waiting, having dismissed his entire staff and replaced them with silent, non-local servants to shield Daisy from gossip. During a stifling lunch, Daisy openly kisses Gatsby and declares her love, shocking Tom, who challenges Gatsby’s claim of attending Oxford and mocks his background. Tensions erupt as Tom forces Gatsby to switch cars: Tom drives Gatsby’s yellow coupé while Gatsby takes Tom’s blue coupé with Daisy, leaving the narrator and Jordan with Tom. The group drives through the oppressive heat to Wilson’s garage in the Valley of Ashes, where Myrtle Wilson, mistaking Jordan for Tom’s wife, watches them with jealous terror. They continue to the Plaza Hotel in New York, where Tom exposes Gatsby’s bootlegging ties to Meyer Wolfsheim; Gatsby demands Daisy renounce Tom, but she reluctantly admits she once loved both men before withdrawing entirely. Tom orders them to return to Long Island in Gatsby’s car. Later, Myrtle rushes out of Wilson’s garage and is struck and killed by a speeding yellow car that flees; Michaelis witnesses the aftermath as Wilson, in shock, cries 'Oh, my God!' Tom and the narrator arrive at the scene, where Tom denies involvement and insists his blue coupé was not the car, but breaks down in tears afterward, calling the driver a coward. That night, the narrator refuses to enter the Buchanans’ house and waits outside, where Gatsby reveals Daisy was driving the fatal car and vows to take the blame. Through the pantry window, Nick sees Tom and Daisy sitting together at the kitchen table, sharing food in quiet, intimate communion—unaware that Daisy has already reconciled with Tom, leaving Gatsby standing alone in the moonlight, still guarding a house that no longer needs him.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Nick Carraway</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Jay Gatsby</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Daisy Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Jordan Baker</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">George Wilson</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Myrtle Wilson</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Michaelis</span>\n                    \n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">+4 more</span>\n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 6: VIII</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">4,530 words &middot; 30m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter unfolds across multiple locations and timelines, beginning with the narrator waking at dawn to Gatsby’s dejection after waiting all night for Daisy, who briefly appeared at her window before extinguishing her light. In Gatsby’s vast, dusty mansion, the two smoke on the French windows as the narrator urges Gatsby to flee, but Gatsby refuses, clinging to the belief that Daisy never truly loved Tom and that their separation was merely 'personal.' He recounts his youth as a penniless officer courting Daisy, his mistaken assumption of social parity, and his devastating return to Louisville after her wedding, walking the streets haunted by memories he could not reclaim. Meanwhile, on the same morning, George Wilson, consumed by grief and delusion after Myrtle’s death, traces the yellow car to Gatsby’s mansion, having learned Gatsby’s name, while the narrator, in the city, receives a strained call from Jordan Baker and fails to reach Gatsby by phone, marking the 3:50 train as his departure. Later, Gatsby, having lost faith in his dream, puts on his bathing suit, retrieves a pneumatic mattress from the garage, and waits by the pool for a call that never comes; at dawn, he is shot by Wilson, who then turns the gun on himself, both bodies discovered by the narrator and staff amid the silent, leaf-strewn pool and grass.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Narrator</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Daisy</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom Buchanan</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Jordan Baker</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">George Wilson</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Michaelis</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Myrtle Wilson</span>\n                    \n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n            <div class=\"chapter-card\">\n                <h4>\n                    <span>Chapter 7: IX</span>\n                    <span class=\"meta\">8,131 words &middot; 54m</span>\n                </h4>\n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">In the aftermath of Gatsby’s death, his mansion is swarmed by police, photographers, and reporters, while false narratives shaped by a detective’s label of Wilson as a 'madman' and Catherine’s false testimony at the inquest erase Gatsby’s connection to Myrtle. The narrator, overwhelmed by guilt, desperately tries to reach Daisy and Meyer Wolfsheim by phone and letter but receives no response, culminating in a chilling long-distance call from Chicago that mistakenly assumes Gatsby is alive—until the narrator interrupts to reveal his death, and the line disconnects. Three days later, Gatsby’s father, Henry C. Gatz, arrives by train from Minnesota, visibly shaken and bundled in a cheap ulster, displaying a worn photograph of Gatsby’s house and a childhood book with a meticulously handwritten self-improvement schedule, only to find that nearly all of Gatsby’s former guests—including Klipspringer, who calls for his tennis shoes, and Wolfsheim, who refuses to attend—abandon the funeral, leaving only Gatz and a Lutheran minister to stand beside the grave in a drizzling rain, where an unexpected visitor, the man with owl-eyed glasses, arrives splashing through mud, murmurs 'Amen to that' after hearing 'Blessed are the dead that the rain falls on,' and quietly calls Gatsby 'the poor son-of-a-bitch.' The narrator, haunted by the East’s moral decay and viewing West Egg as a grotesque El Greco painting, recalls his childhood train journeys through the Midwest’s winter nights, defined by sleigh bells and frosty street lamps, and decides to return home; he meets Jordan Baker, who reveals she is engaged to another man, and they exchange bitter words about dishonesty, ending with his admission that he is too old to lie to himself. On a late October afternoon, he confronts Tom Buchanan on Fifth Avenue, who admits he told Wilson that Gatsby owned the car that killed Myrtle, justifying it as retribution, and emotionally recalls crying over dog biscuits left in his flat; the narrator shakes Tom’s hand, feeling as if speaking to a child, as Tom enters a jewelry store to buy a pearl necklace or cuff buttons. The narrator visits Gatsby’s abandoned mansion one final time, erasing an obscene word scrawled on the white steps, avoids the taxi driver linked to the accident, and walks to the beach to watch the moon rise, reflecting on the lost beauty of the original island and Gatsby’s futile pursuit of the green light, ending with the meditation that humanity is forever striving forward while being borne back ceaselessly into the past.</div>\n                \n                \n                <div class=\"chapter-characters\" style=\"margin-top: 0.75rem;\">\n                    <strong style=\"margin-right: 0.5rem; color: var(--muted);\">Characters:</strong>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Gatsby</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Wilson</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Catherine</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Michaelis</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Meyer Wolfsheim</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Daisy</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Tom</span>\n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">Narrator</span>\n                    \n                    \n                    <span class=\"tag\">+7 more</span>\n                    \n                </div>\n                \n            </div>\n            \n        </section>\n        </div>\n\n        <div id=\"characters\" class=\"tab-content\">\n        <section>\n            <h2>👥 Character Guide</h2>\n\n            <!-- Confidence Filter -->\n            <div style=\"margin-bottom: 1.5rem;\">\n                <label style=\"color: var(--muted); margin-right: 0.5rem;\">\n                    <input type=\"checkbox\" id=\"show-low-confidence-only\" onchange=\"filterByConfidence(this.checked)\">\n                    Show only low-confidence items\n                </label>\n            </div>\n\n            \n            <div class=\"character-group\">\n                <h3>Main Characters <span class=\"count\">12</span></h3>\n\n                \n                <div class=\"character-profile character-item\" data-confidence=\"low\">\n                    <h4>\n                        Gatsby\n                        \n                        \n                        <span class=\"confidence-badge low\" title=\"Confidence: low\">\n                            ⚠\n                            \n                        </span>\n                    </h4>\n\n                    \n                    <div class=\"aliases-list\" style=\"margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\">Also known as: Mr. Gatsby</div>\n                    \n\n                    \n                    \n\n                    \n\n                    \n\n                    \n                    \n\n                    \n                    \n                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Gatsby is a mysterious and enigmatic figure whose wealth and lavish parties conceal a deep personal longing, particularly for Daisy Buchanan. He presents himself as charming and courteous, yet his actions—such as associating with questionable figures and maintaining an elaborate facade—reveal a morally ambiguous nature, blending idealism with questionable means.</div>\n                    \n\n                    \n                    \n\n                    \n                    <details style=\"margin-top: 0.5rem;\">\n                        <summary style=\"cursor: pointer; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.85rem;\">📊 Metadata</summary>\n                        <div class=\"profile-meta\" style=\"margin-top: 0.5rem; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--muted);\">\n                            <span>259 mentions</span> •\n                            <span>First appears: Ch. 1</span> •\n                            <span class=\"tag low\">low confidence</span>\n                        </div>\n                    </details>\n                </div>\n                \n                <div class=\"character-profile character-item\" data-confidence=\"high\